You can generate your own FIX code classes in QuickFIX/J by customizing the XML FIX dictionary and running the QuickFIX/J code generator (MessageCodeGenerator). This lets you add custom fields, define new messages, or extend existing ones, and the generator will produce strongly‑typed Java classes for you. deepwiki.com quickfixj.org

🔎 Key Steps to Generate Custom FIX Classes
1. Prepare a Custom FIX Dictionary
* QuickFIX/J uses XML data dictionaries (e.g., FIX44.xml) to define fields, components, and messages.
* To add your own:
    * Copy the base dictionary (e.g., FIX44.xml).
    * Add custom fields: <field number="9001" name="CustomField" type="STRING"/>
    * 
    * Add custom messages: <message name="CustomMessage" msgtype="Z" msgcat="app">
    *   <field name="CustomField" required="Y"/>
    * </message>
    * 
* Save as CUSTOM_FIX.xml.

2. Run the QuickFIX/J Code Generator
* QuickFIX/J provides quickfix.codegen.MessageCodeGenerator.
* Example Maven plugin configuration: <plugin>
*   <groupId>org.quickfixj</groupId>
*   <artifactId>quickfixj-codegenerator</artifactId>
*   <version>2.3.0</version>
*   <executions>
*     <execution>
*       <goals>
*         <goal>generate</goal>
*       </goals>
*       <configuration>
*         <specification>/path/to/CUSTOM_FIX.xml</specification>
*         <transformDirectory>${project.build.directory}/generated-sources</transformDirectory>
*       </configuration>
*     </execution>
*   </executions>
* </plugin>
* 
* This generates:
    * Message classes (CustomMessage.java)
    * Field classes (CustomField.java)
    * Component classes
    * Factory classes

3. Use Generated Classes in Your Code
* After generation, include the classes in your project: import quickfix.field.CustomField;
* import quickfix.fix44.CustomMessage;
* 
* CustomMessage msg = new CustomMessage();
* msg.set(new CustomField("HelloWorld"));
* session.send(msg);
* 
* You now have type‑safe access to your custom FIX fields and messages.

4. Advanced Customization
* You can define code generation tasks (MessageCodeGenerator.Task) to:
    * Change package names.
    * Apply XSLT transforms for custom output.
    * Integrate with FIX Orchestra for richer metadata deepwiki.com quickfixj.org.

📊 Comparison of Approaches
Approach	Pros	Cons
Modify existing FIX dictionary	Simple, quick, works with standard generator	May diverge from official FIX spec
Create new custom dictionary	Full control, supports new messages/fields	Requires maintaining schema evolution
Use FIX Orchestra	Rich metadata, schema evolution support	More complex setup
⚠️ Risks & Considerations
* Schema evolution: If you add fields/messages, ensure both counterparties agree on the custom FIX spec.
* Versioning: Keep track of dictionary versions to avoid mismatches.
* Testing: Always validate generated classes against a FIX simulator or counterparty before production.

✅ In practice: Most teams start by modifying FIX44.xml or FIX50SP2.xml to add custom fields/messages, then run the QuickFIX/J code generator to produce Java classes. This gives you type‑safe FIX messages tailored to your business needs.

